@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Non-Clustered Index";
}

<div class="container-fluid px-4 py-4">

    <!-- =========================
         PAGE HEADER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="mb-4">

        <h1 class="fw-bold">
            Non-Clustered Index
        </h1>

        <p class="text-muted">
            A Non-Clustered Index is a separate structure that helps SQL Server
            find data quickly without changing the physical order of the table.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         INTRODUCTION
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                What is a Non-Clustered Index?
            </h2>

            <p>
                A <strong>Non-Clustered Index</strong> is an index structure
                that is stored separately from the actual table data.
            </p>

            <p>
                It contains the indexed column values along with a reference
                that allows SQL Server to locate the actual rows in the table.
            </p>

            <p>
                Unlike a clustered index, a non-clustered index does not
                determine the physical order of the table data.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         CLUSTERED VS NON-CLUSTERED
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-info mb-4">

        <h4 class="fw-bold">
            Important Difference
        </h4>

        <p>
            A clustered index determines the physical order of table data,
            whereas a non-clustered index is a separate structure that
            contains indexed values and row references.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SYNTAX
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Syntax
            </h2>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE NONCLUSTERED INDEX index_name
ON table_name(column_name);</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         CREATE TABLE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Example Table
            </h2>

            <p>
                Consider the following Students table:
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE TABLE Students
(
    StudentId INT,
    StudentName VARCHAR(100),
    Course VARCHAR(100),
    City VARCHAR(50),
    Age INT
);</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         CREATING NON-CLUSTERED INDEX
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Creating a Non-Clustered Index
            </h2>

            <p>
                Suppose we frequently search students based on the
                <strong>City</strong> column.
            </p>

            <p>
                We can create a non-clustered index on the City column:
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE NONCLUSTERED INDEX IX_Students_City
ON Students(City);</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Here:
            </p>

            <ul>

                <li>
                    <strong>IX_Students_City</strong> is the index name.
                </li>

                <li>
                    <strong>Students</strong> is the table name.
                </li>

                <li>
                    <strong>City</strong> is the indexed column.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SAMPLE DATA
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Sample Data
            </h2>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>INSERT INTO Students
VALUES
(101, 'Rahul', 'Dot Net', 'Pune', 22),
(102, 'Priya', 'Java', 'Mumbai', 21),
(103, 'Amit', 'Python', 'Pune', 23),
(104, 'Neha', 'Testing', 'Delhi', 22),
(105, 'Rohit', 'Dot Net', 'Pune', 24);</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SELECT QUERY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Using the Non-Clustered Index
            </h2>

            <p>
                Now suppose we want to find all students from Pune:
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
WHERE City = 'Pune';</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                If SQL Server determines that the non-clustered index is
                useful for the query, it can use the index to locate the
                required rows efficiently.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         MULTIPLE NON-CLUSTERED INDEXES
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Multiple Non-Clustered Indexes
            </h2>

            <p>
                Unlike a clustered index, a table can have multiple
                non-clustered indexes.
            </p>

            <p>
                For example, we can create indexes on both
                <strong>City</strong> and <strong>Course</strong>.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE NONCLUSTERED INDEX IX_Students_City
ON Students(City);

CREATE NONCLUSTERED INDEX IX_Students_Course
ON Students(Course);</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Both indexes can exist on the same table.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         MULTIPLE COLUMN INDEX
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Non-Clustered Index on Multiple Columns
            </h2>

            <p>
                A non-clustered index can also contain more than one column.
                Such an index is called a composite or multi-column index.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE NONCLUSTERED INDEX IX_Students_City_Course
ON Students(City, Course);</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                This index contains both <strong>City</strong> and
                <strong>Course</strong> columns.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         INCLUDE COLUMNS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Including Additional Columns
            </h2>

            <p>
                SQL Server also allows additional non-key columns to be
                included in a non-clustered index.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE NONCLUSTERED INDEX IX_Students_City
ON Students(City)
INCLUDE(StudentName, Course);</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Here, <strong>City</strong> is the index key and
                <strong>StudentName</strong> and <strong>Course</strong>
                are included columns.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         ADVANTAGES
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Advantages of Non-Clustered Index
            </h2>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    A table can have multiple non-clustered indexes.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    They can improve search performance.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    They are useful for frequently searched columns.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    They can improve queries using WHERE conditions.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    They can be useful for JOIN and ORDER BY operations.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Included columns can help cover frequently executed queries.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         DISADVANTAGES
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Disadvantages of Non-Clustered Index
            </h2>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Non-clustered indexes require additional storage.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Too many indexes can slow down INSERT operations.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    UPDATE and DELETE operations may require index maintenance.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Indexes need to be maintained as table data changes.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         CLUSTERED VS NON-CLUSTERED
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-4">
                Clustered Index vs Non-Clustered Index
            </h2>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-dark">

                        <tr>
                            <th>Clustered Index</th>
                            <th>Non-Clustered Index</th>
                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>
                            <td>
                                Determines the physical order of table data.
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Does not determine the physical order of table data.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>
                                Only one clustered index can exist on a table.
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Multiple non-clustered indexes can exist on a table.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>
                                Data rows are associated directly with the
                                clustered index order.
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                The index contains keys and references to
                                the actual rows.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>
                                Useful for range-based retrieval.
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Useful for targeted searches and filtering.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT POINTS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-warning mb-4">

        <h4 class="fw-bold">
            Important Points
        </h4>

        <ul class="mb-0">

            <li>
                A table can have multiple non-clustered indexes.
            </li>

            <li>
                A non-clustered index is stored separately from the
                actual table data.
            </li>

            <li>
                Non-clustered indexes can improve SELECT query performance.
            </li>

            <li>
                Indexes require additional storage space.
            </li>

            <li>
                Too many indexes can negatively affect INSERT, UPDATE
                and DELETE operations.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="p-4 rounded shadow-sm mb-4"
         style="background-color:#cfe2ff;">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Summary
        </h2>

        <p>
            A <strong>Non-Clustered Index</strong> is a separate index
            structure that helps SQL Server locate rows efficiently.
            It does not change the physical order of data in the table.
        </p>

        <p>
            Unlike a clustered index, a table can contain multiple
            non-clustered indexes.
        </p>

        <p class="mb-0">
            Non-clustered indexes are useful for frequently searched,
            filtered, joined and sorted columns, but they should be
            created carefully because they require additional storage
            and maintenance.
        </p>

    </div>


